  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 242 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mostly dry conditions are expected today into tonight. \nModest radiational cooling will be offset by incoming high clouds, \novernight. Mid 50s to mid 60s are expected early tomorrow morning, \nwith perhaps a few cool hollows in the upper 40s. On Sunday, surface \nhigh pressure that has locked our region beneath a dry, stable air \nmass will shift farther out to sea. High temperatures will rise into \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s for Sunday. Increasing moisture is \nexpected, mainly over northern New York while a subtle trough angles \nsouth of the Adirondacks. 250-750 J/kg of CAPE will develop with \ndaytime heating with weak flow aloft. Isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorm will be possible across northern New York, while \nVermont will remain mostly dry. Showers will likely favor terrain \nboundaries, drift off their point of initiation, and generally \ndecay. With slow-movers, a few brief downpours could occur. With the \nloss of daylight, shower activity will decrease, though perhaps not \nentirely 0 percent. Mid 50s to mid 60s are expected still.\n\nMonday will be a rinse-and-repeat, but everything will have shifted \nslightly east. With increasing surface dewpoints, we could eke out \ncloser to 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE, but there will be little shear \npresent. Northern New York will remain the primary focus for \nconvection, with fewer cells across Vermont. Some surface confluence \nwill be present, but there won't be any upper feature. Convection \nwill remain garden variety with somewhat higher potential for \nlocalized downpours due to the greater extent of rain and slowly \nincreasing PWATs.\n\nThen Tuesday will also be largely the same. A stronger upper level \nvort will approach from the west, but its influence won't creep into \nthe area until relatively late on Tuesday or even Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday. Forecast scenarios diverge, with the main \ndifferences being the strength and intensity of the incoming surface \nlow. Most guidance favors a deep, sub 1000mb surface low closing off \nnear Lake Ontario with the occlusion process resulting in briefly \nerratic motions before it shuffles east. A minority favors a weaker \nsystem with a surface low not developing until the upper trough is \nmoving offshore. All told, there will be chances for showers and \nthunderstorms with little change to shear or instability regardless \nof which forecast scenario you pick. Localized downpours will be \npossible, mainly wherever surface convergence aligns with increasing \nupper divergence in association with the developing upper low.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An upper level low builds into the region for middle and \nlate week and brings periods of rain. It looks to move slowly from \nwest to east Tuesday through Thursday, and the time of its exit is \nstill very uncertain. A relatively potent surface low should form \nalong it and bring an area of consistent rainfall, but its exact \nposition is uncertain. The solution favored by most EPS members puts \nthe bullseye over central/southern Vermont, where probabilities of \n2, 3 and 4 inches of rain are 50-70, 20-50 and 10-20 percent \nrespectively. This could lead to a flood threat that would need\nto be watched carefully. A saving grace is that the precipitation\nwon't be as convective after Monday, something that would cut \ndown on rainfall rates. PWATs look to be in the 1.3-1.5 inch \nrange, sufficient for heavy rain but not overly high as the \ntrough is northern stream. The GEFS/CMCE ensembles favor the \ntrack of the surface low over coastal New England and keep the \nprobability of 3 inches under 10 percent, and the probability of\n2 inches in the 10-40 percent range, something less concerning \ngiven the relatively long time of the precip and lower rates. \nThe rain should become more scattered and diurnally driven late \nweek and into the weekend, but isolated to scattered afternoon \nshowers will still be possible.",
